1. Art direction

Zixxtrixx is not a realistic snake.

He is a strange, theatrical, slightly ridiculous creature whose personality includes the escalating click lines:

“I have one trick.”
“I have two tricks.”
…
“I have Zixxtrixx.”

That personality should inform the motion. He can be flamboyant, smug and physically excessive. His attack does not need zoological plausibility.

The concept’s critical visual identity is:

upright, compact S-shaped posture;
front of body raised strongly from the ground;
broad, unusual head rather than a simple capsule;
enormous integrated yellow eyes;
blue head and throat;
green body;
pink dorsal region and tail markings;
orange eye surround;
broad, colourful forked tail forms;
handmade crayon/pencil surface rather than clean plastic.

The existing horizontal ground-snake silhouette is rejected.

2. Rebuild the static model before touching the final animation

First produce a canonical static model that matches the concept.

Create fixed front and side renders using a nearly orthographic camera aligned to the concept sheets. Display the concept and render side by side, and also produce black-silhouette comparisons.

Match in this order:

overall upright S silhouette;
head-to-body proportion;
eye size, location and integration into the skull;
position of the major body curves;
body thickness and taper;
forked tail outline;
colour-region boundaries;
small details.

Do not begin by adding surface detail to the existing horizontal tube.

Required shape changes
Raise the front half substantially.
Give the body a permanent upright S-shaped canonical stance.
Make the skull broad, flattened and designed in silhouette.
Do not represent the head as another rotationally symmetric ring sausage.
Integrate the eyes into the head contour; they should influence the silhouette rather than looking glued onto it.
Make the tail ends broader, flatter and more leaf-, blade- or fin-like. Avoid three narrow tapered horns unless the concept comparison proves that shape.
Restore the mouth as texture or low-cost geometry.
Restore the orange eye surround.
Restore the blue-to-green throat transition.
Let the pink dorsal region follow the body as a painted region instead of reading as a separate helmet and a chain of attached pink tubes.
Do not treat RingPart as a hardware limit

The ring-cylinder authoring path is an offline tool convenience. The hardware sees compiled meshlets.

Use offset and elliptical rings where they are sufficient. Add a custom-mesh or generalized-profile path on the tooling side where circular rings cannot reproduce the concept. Do not redesign silicon for this.

Start LOD0 around 10–12 body sides, with more where the head and eyes actually require it, then measure. Six sides were chosen to appease the GIF palette budget and must not survive by inertia.

Use smooth normals across continuous organic surfaces. Retain intentional hard edges on the tail blades or other designed forms. “More smooth” must not mean glossy generic fantasy plastic; the crayon texture will carry the handmade roughness.

3. Replace the segmented body with a continuous skinned shell

The current body is made from independent rigid cylinder pieces. That is why the attack opens visible cracks.

Texture cannot cover a literal geometric hole.

Build one continuous body surface:

shared boundary vertices between longitudinal sections;
no caps between body segments;
enough intermediate rings to hold the curved silhouette;
joint rings weighted across adjacent bones;
no more than two bone influences per vertex;
continuous UV coordinates along the entire body;
texture seam placed underneath the creature, not along a visible side;
neck/body and body/tail transitions either share topology or overlap invisibly inside a thicker form.

Near a joint, blend vertices between the two adjacent bones. In the middle of a segment, one dominant bone is fine. Preserve the existing ≤32-bone law.

An overlapping-segment fix may be used for a quick diagnostic, but it is not the final answer. The reusable asset-tool primitive should be a continuous flexible chain suitable for snakes, tails, tentacles, long necks and similar creatures.

Add checks for:

weight count ≤2;
normalized weights;
shared seam positions;
UV continuity;
no internal segment caps;
no visible opening in the most extreme attack pose.
4. Start the real creature texture pipeline

Zixxtrixx must become the first vertical slice through an actual reusable creature-texture pipeline.

Do not make a Zixxtrixx-only hack, but do not turn this into a year-long general-purpose DCC project either. Build the minimum general path needed to ship this creature properly.

The path should cover:

source texture asset and metadata;
UVs on ring-generated and custom geometry;
deterministic conversion into formats already supported by the machine;
palette generation where CLUT8 is used;
mip generation;
cartridge/page packing;
reference-renderer sampling;
reel and website consumption;
validation and budget reporting.

Follow the existing TMU and creature contracts rather than inventing an unrelated texture system. CLUT8 is probably a good fit for the hand-drawn creature surface, with nearest sampling and proper mips. Compare it against direct RGB565 only after measuring quality and memory.

Crayon texture direction

The surface should visibly come from the drawings:

uneven crayon or pencil coverage;
overlapping strokes;
subtle paper grain;
darker pressure marks;
slight colour variation inside each large region;
imperfect hand-drawn boundaries;
no photoreal scales;
no procedural reptile skin;
no clean plastic gradients.

Use several shared pages rather than a unique texture for every tiny rigid piece. A sensible starting decomposition is:

continuous body page;
head/throat page;
tail page;
eye page or eye material.

The body texture must flow continuously over joints. Use longitudinal UVs so the pink dorsal marking follows the full body. The current “texture runs around the ring while the stripe runs along the animal” limitation must be removed in the authoring/compiler path.

Generate palette-aware mips that preserve the major blue, green, pink, yellow and orange identity at distance. Do not let the smallest mip turn the creature into green-brown mud.

