yellow eyes;
orange border;
green body;
pink dorsal/tail accents.
6. Replace the slither with strong caterpillar-like locomotion

The current side-to-side sine wave is visually negligible and conceptually wrong.

The locomotion should be primarily vertical and longitudinal, not lateral:

the elevated front anchors;
a strong arch travels through the body;
the middle rises and bunches;
the rear is drawn forward;
the body extends and settles;
root motion advances the animal.

Think caterpillar or inchworm more than snake.

The up/down motion should be exaggerated enough to read instantly at 384×240. A viewer should not need to stare at the contour to discover that an animation exists.

Use pitch through the X/Y plane as the main movement. A small lateral sway or roll may remain as secondary life, but it should contribute perhaps a fraction of the vertical authority rather than dominating it.

Requirements:

preserve the upright S-shaped head posture;
keep the head alert and intentionally controlled;
make the body visibly compress and release;
avoid ground penetration;
avoid obvious foot-sliding equivalents;
produce a clean loop;
make at least two full visible cycles available in the website preview.

A fixed-camera side or three-quarter render must clearly show the gait before any orbit camera is applied.

7. Make the tail strike absurdly dynamic

The current attack is an engineering sketch. Replace it with something theatrically excessive.

The first serious prototype should be a triple salto mortale tail attack:

Zixxtrixx compresses and coils.
The front braces and the rear rises.
Tail and body make the first overhead rotation.
A second, faster rotation builds momentum.
A third rotation becomes the committed attack.
The tail prongs spear downward in front of the head.
Hold the impact briefly.
Recoil, shake off the force and return to stance.

Prototype at least two versions:

head/front acts as the pivot while the rear and tail perform three overhead loops;
the whole creature becomes a compact forward-somersaulting wheel before uncoiling into the stab.

Choose based on readability, not realism.

The whole animal should participate. Do not simply rotate the last three tail bones.

Eliminate the current animation discontinuity

Do not switch a joint abruptly between lateral yaw and attack pitch based on a threshold.

Compose pitch, yaw and roll continuously using proper quaternion composition or an equivalent deterministic authoring helper. Use smooth authored curves for anticipation, acceleration, impact and recovery.

The attack needs:

a clear anticipation silhouette;
accelerating motion;
a precise contact pose;
an attack event on the actual contact frame;
a short impact hold or hit-stop;
visible follow-through;
no cracks;
no sudden axis popping;
a loop or return whose last pose does not jump into the first pose.

It may be longer than the current attack. Longer is desirable when it improves readability.

8. Re-evaluate 30 Hz held poses

The current rule uses 30 Hz keys held for two 60 Hz simulation ticks without interpolation. Do not assume that remains visually sufficient merely because it came from the donor.

First remove the axis discontinuity and slow the preview camera. Then create an A/B comparison:

current 30 Hz held-pose playback;
deterministic 60 Hz presentation interpolation between the same stored keys.

A fixed-point normalized linear quaternion blend is a plausible experiment. Do not commit a hardware redesign before the visual comparison.

If interpolation makes the triple strike and falling motion materially better, document the evidence and amend the relevant creature/pose plan before producing fifty creatures. A per-clip interpolation flag is acceptable if ordinary locomotion does not need it but fast attacks and falling do.

The game remains deterministic; this is presentation interpolation, not gameplay truth.

